.TH PPING 1 "September 2026" "xcat-ports 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pping \- parallel ping utility for cluster nodes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pping
[\fB\-i\fR|\fB\-\-interface\fR \fIinterfaces\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-use_fping\fR]
[\fB\-X\fR|\fB\-\-noexpand\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR]
\fInoderange\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pping
is a standalone port of xCAT's \fBpping\fR utility. It expands a noderange
locally (without requiring \fBxcatd\fR or an xCAT database) and tests the
network reachability of multiple nodes in parallel.
.PP
By default, \fBpping\fR uses \fBnmap\fR(1) if available on the system
(invoking: \fBnmap \-PE \-\-system\-dns \-\-send\-ip \-sP \-\-unprivileged \-PA80,443,22\fR).
If \fBnmap\fR is not found or when \fB\-f\fR is specified, it falls back to
\fBfping\fR(1).
.PP
Output is printed line by line:
.IP
.nf
\fInode\fR: ping
\fInode\fR: noping
.fi
.PP
Responsive nodes are reported in discovery order as responses arrive. Unreachable
nodes are printed sorted at the end.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-use_fping\fR
Force using \fBfping\fR(1) instead of \fBnmap\fR(1).
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interface\fR \fIinterfaces\fR
Probe specific network interface suffixes. \fIinterfaces\fR is a comma-separated
list of interface names (e.g. \fBeth0,ib0\fR). The interface name is appended as
a hyphenated suffix to each node name (e.g. \fBnode1\-eth0\fR). Any existing
\fB\-hf\fR\fIN\fR suffix on the node name is stripped before appending.
.TP
\fB\-X\fR, \fB\-\-noexpand\fR
Do not expand the noderange; treat the argument as an already expanded,
comma-separated list of hosts.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.SH NODERANGE SYNTAX
The \fInoderange\fR expression supports full local xCAT noderange syntax:
.TP
.B node1,node2,node3
Explicit list of nodes (comma-separated).
.TP
.B node[1-100], node[001-200]
Bracket ranges, preserving zero-padding.
.TP
.B f[1-2]n[1-3]
Multiple bracket groups in a single expression.
.TP
.B node1:node200, rack1-rack4
Hyphen/colon range between two node names differing in exactly one numeric field.
.TP
.B node10+3
Numeric increment shorthand (expands to \fBnode10,node11,node12,node13\fR).
.TP
.B 10-12
Numbers without prefix default to prefix \fBnode\fR (configurable via \fBXCAT_NODE_PREFIX\fR).
.TP
.B 192.168.0.[1-50]
IPv4 bracket ranges.
.TP
.B ,-node3, -(node2,node4)
Exclusion operators. Applied after inclusions.
.TP
.B group1@group2
Intersection operator.
.TP
.B ^/path/to/file
Read node names from a file (one per line or space/colon delimited; '#' comments supported).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (all targets were pingable).
.TP
.B 1
One or more nodes were unreachable, or invalid arguments were provided.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ping a numbered sequence of compute nodes:
.IP
.B pping node[1-64]
.PP
Ping nodes using fping explicitly:
.IP
.B pping -f rack1-rack4
.PP
Ping secondary interfaces:
.IP
.B pping -i eth1,ib0 node[1-10]
.PP
Ping an IP range:
.IP
.B pping '192.168.100.[1-50]'
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xdsh (1),
.BR fping (1),
.BR nmap (1)
